===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|dubben}}, from {{inh|en|ang|dubbian|t=to knight by striking with a sword, dub}} and {{der|en|fro|adober|t=to equip with arms; adorn}} (also 11th century, Modern {{cog|fr|adouber}}, both from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*dubbōn}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*dub-|t=to fit, strike}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*dʰewbʰ-|t=plug, peg, wedge}}.

Cognate with {{cog|is|dubba}} (''dubba til riddara''). Compare also {{m|en|drub}} for an English reflex of the Germanic word.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|now|historical|or|ceremonial}} To confer [[knighthood]]; the conclusion of the ceremony was marked by a tap on the shoulder with a sword, the [[accolade]].
#: {{syn|en|knight}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-3|act=II|scene=ii|page=154|column=2|passage=You promiſt Knighthood to our forward ſonne, / Vnſheath your ſword, and '''dub''' him preſently.}}
#* {{RQ:Cervantes Ormsby Don Quixote|volume=I|page=114|chapter=II|passage=It occurred to him that he had not been '''dubbed''' a knight, and that according to the law of chivalry he neither could nor ought to bear arms against any knight; {{...}}.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[name]], to [[entitle]], to [[call]]. {{defdate|from the later 16th c.}}
#: {{syn|en|designate|name|Thesaurus:denominate}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Tom Sawyer|passage=They tripped along the murky aisles with the rest of the company, visiting the familiar wonders of the cave—wonders '''dubbed''' with rather over-descriptive names, such as “The Drawing-Room,” “The Cathedral,” Aladdin’s Palace,” and so on.}}
#* {{RQ:Chambers Younger Set|V|passage=As a matter of fact its narrow ornate façade presented not a single quiet space that the eyes might rest on after a tiring attempt to follow and codify the arabesques, foliations, and intricate vermiculations of what some disrespectfully '''dubbed''' as “near-aissance.”}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=70|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579879-buy-out-firm-really-does-focus-operational-improvements-engineers Engineers of a different kind]
|passage=Private-equity nabobs bristle at being '''dubbed''' mere financiers. Piling debt onto companies’ balance-sheets is only a small part of what leveraged buy-outs are about, they insist. Improving the workings of the businesses they take over is just as core to their calling, if not more so. Much of their pleading is public-relations bluster.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2023 December 27|author=Stephen Roberts|title=Bradshaw's Britain: the way to Weymouth|journal=RAIL|issue=999|page=52|text=Stephen reigned from 1135-1154, that nasty period of our history '''dubbed''' 'The Anarchy', when forces loyal to Stephen contested the throne with those of Henry I's daughter Matilda, who by rights should have been queen. Stephen, her cousin, plonked his own posterior on the throne.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[deem]].
#: {{syn|en|consider|think of|Thesaurus:deem}}
#* {{RQ:Pope Horace|passage=A man of wealth is '''dubbed''' a man of worth.}}
# To [[clothe]] or [[invest]]; to [[ornament]]; to [[adorn]].
#: {{syn|en|deck out|embellish|Thesaurus:decorate}}
#* {{RQ:Malory Le Morte Darthur|passage=His diadem was dropped down / '''Dubbed''' with stones.}}
# {{lb|en|heading}} ''To strike, rub, or dress smooth; to dab.''
## To dress with an [[adze]].
##: {{ux|en|to '''dub''' a stick of timber smooth}}
## To strike [[cloth]] with [[teasel]]s to raise a [[nap]].
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1808|title=Annual Register|passage=For dressing or '''dubbing''' cloths, either wet or dry, otherwise than by green cards and pickards}}
## To rub or dress with grease, as leather in the process of [[curry]]ing it.
##* '''1852-1866''', {{w|Charles Tomlinson (scientist)|Charles Tomlinson}}, ''Cyclopaedia of Useful Arts and Manufactures''
##*: When the skin is thoroughly cleansed, and while yet in its wet and distended state, the process of stuffing, or '''dubbing''' (probably a corruption of daubing), is performed. Both sides of the skin, but chiefly the flesh side, are smeared or daubed with a mixture of cod-oil and tallow
## To dress a fishing fly.
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1689|author=James Chetham|title=The Anglers Vade Mecum|passage=if you can dub a Fly of the exact colour of the Natural Fly, Fish at that instant take, it's sufficient}}
# To prepare (a [[gamecock]]) for fighting, by trimming the [[hackle]]s and cutting off the [[comb]] and [[wattle]]s.

